<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Victorinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 271</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Marcus Piavonius Victorinus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Victorinus was emperor of the breakaway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a> from 269 to 271. A senior officer under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postumus">Postumus</a>, he took power after the short reign of Marius and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> returned to the central empire. He was murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in 271 by one of his officers, reportedly over a private grievance, and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetricus-i">Tetricus I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victorinus.jpg" alt="Victorinus" width="292" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omicroñ'R · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aureus_Victorinus_RIC_0094_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marcus Piavonius Victorinus came from a wealthy Gallic family and served as a soldier under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postumus">Postumus</a>, the first of the Gallic emperors. He showed considerable ability. He held the post of tribune of the praetorians in 266 or 267 and rose swiftly to become co-consul with Postumus in 268, and Postumus may then have made him praetorian prefect. Shortly after Postumus put down the rebellion of Laelianus in 269, his own troops murdered him and appointed Marius emperor in his place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After engineering the death of Marius, Victorinus was declared emperor by the troops at Augusta Treverorum, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, in the autumn of 269. His chief concern was to keep the western provinces from submitting to the central Roman government, but in his first weeks only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> recognised him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> deserted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a> and declared for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius Gothicus</a>, who sent his general Placidianus to south-eastern Gaul. Placidianus took Cularo, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a>, but went no further.</p><p>His presence led the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Augustodunum</a>, now Autun, to abandon Victorinus and announce for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. Victorinus marched south and besieged it; it fell after seven months, and his troops plundered and destroyed it. He returned in triumph to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Why Claudius did not allow Placidianus to relieve Autun is unknown, but it is speculated that he was fully engaged against the Alamanni in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and did not want a second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Victorinus was murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in early 271 by one of his officers, Attitianus, whose wife he had supposedly seduced. Because the motive was personal and not political, his mother, Victoria, whom some sources call Vitruvia, was able to keep power. She arranged his deification and, after a large payment to the troops, the appointment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetricus-i">Tetricus I</a> as his successor. Another commander, apparently proclaimed as Domitianus II, was soon eliminated.</p><p>The Historia Augusta, a dubious source, lists Victorinus among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">Thirty Tyrants</a>. It also describes a Victorinus Junior, allegedly his son, whom the family proclaimed emperor on the day of his father's murder and whom the troops killed immediately; it says both were buried in marble tombs near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Victorinus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Postumus was murdered by his troops in 269, followed by Marius, <b>Victorinus</b> and Tetricus I.</blockquote><a href="/state/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonia-claudia-ara-agrippinensium">Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=261" aria-label="Gaul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4618 529 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Victorinus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5068 799h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5068 799h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=261">What was here in 261 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
